General rules for .rst documentation
------------------------------------

We want to use as little text as possible. Be concise. Do not repeat subjects, use pronouns unless the sentence would be too long. Use enumerations. Or preferably use bullet lists if there is more that a sentence assiciated with an item. 

We don't want to document code structure that should be obvious if someone had a quick glance at the source code. You should prioritize documenting information that is not obvious, like resource constraints, speed optimizations, clock-domain crossing etc.

A lot of information can be obtained from the comments in the VHDL, but if in doubt, is is ok to add a ".. todo::" note to indicate that the information shall be completed.

.rst documents that document a VHDL module shall have the following structure.

- .. title:: <entity_name>
- In a double *** fence :entity:`<entity_name>` *<module_filename>*: <short_description>
- Overview section. Describes the module, unless a sufficiently good description is provided in the entity  
- .. parse:: <module_full_path_filename>: Uses Sphinx plugin to parse VHDL code
- .. toctree:: with :titlesonly: parameter. Add all submodules, and end with separator line.
- Entity Documentation:  Use .. autoentity:: <entity_name> 
- Principle of operations: See section description below.
- Memory Map: If the module has BSB registers, automatically include memory map table with .. include-docs:: command, specifying the search keyword. 


Principle or Operations section
-----------------------

- Generic parameters: Use a bullet list to list the key generic parameters with a description, with limits of their utilization. Multiple parameters could be grouped if they can easily be described together.
 
- Interface: The interfaces shall be described as a bullet list, one bullet per group of signals or per bus. Here are typical interfaces:
   - AXI interfaces (usually start with s_axis_*, m_axis_*). Description of the interface function shall be concise and shall specify the type of bus (AXI4-Streaming), wether it is blocking or non-blocking, and to which case it is synchronous to (e.g. non-blocking AXI4-Streaming synhronout to ``clk``) . The following sub-bullets shall include additional details:
      
      - COntents of TDATA: Total width in bits, or of the TDATA word contains multiple elements, indicate the numbe rof elements and width of elements, usually defined as globals. The order of the element shall be specified (e.g. first sample/first bin/ real/imaginary parts  towards the MSB). In all cases the type of binary encoding shall be specified (2's complement, unsigned). Padding in the MSB or LSB shall be described if applicable.
      - COntents of the TUSER word. Sub-bullet list indicating the meaning of each bit of TUSER. If multiple bits associate to multiple samples/bins in TDATA, indicate that TUSER bit 0 correspond to the sample/bin 0.
      - tvalid/tlast: not necessary to specify them, unless there are unusual restrictions, like tvalid cannot be interrupted once started (FFT module is like that)    

   - BSB control bus: Often defined as CMD_DAT, CMD_RDY, RPLY_DAT, RPLY_RDY structures, but sometimes defined as m_axis_bsb_*. Just indicate it allows access to the module's control and status registers, and RAM space if applicable for that module.


Clocks and resets are described in the following section. Do not describe them here.

- Clocking and reset: 
    - Bullet list decribing the clock inputs (and outputs if applicable, but specify it is an output) with a brief description of what they are used for. Note that ctrl_rst is use for the BSB control bus (might be renamed bsb_clk in the future). 

    - Bullet list of resets, with their description on what they reset, and wether they are synchronous or asynchronous. If this is not obsious, add a todo note.

    Describe cross clock-domain crossing strategy. Use todo notes if this is not clear.



Other sections could be inspired from the file FFT.rst.

